WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.455
Diego Alvarez: Morning, Cody. How's it going down in Austin?

2
00:00:11.284 --> 00:00:20.460
Cody Barrett: Morning, Diego. Pretty good, busy week. We just kicked off the fall campaign for the Phoenix metro, so the phones are ringing more than usual.

3
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.936
Diego Alvarez: Nice. What kind of volume are you seeing now?

4
00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:37.287
Cody Barrett: Across the three states we're doing about 9,200 calls a month, and roughly 60 percent of that is Google Ads, the rest Meta and some organic.

5
00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:39.868
Cody Barrett: Texas is still the biggest chunk.

6
00:00:40.698 --> 00:00:45.614
Diego Alvarez: Good, that's up from the last time we talked. Anything new on the team side?

7
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:57.870
Cody Barrett: Yeah, we added four more seats on the Spanish-speaking floor in San Antonio, so we're at 70 now. Two of them are still in training with Marisol.

8
00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:03.666
Diego Alvarez: Got it. Is the bilingual queue routing the way you want it?

9
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:12.983
Cody Barrett: It's fine, no issues there. The Spanish line gets about 30 percent of the traffic and it's holding steady.

10
00:01:13.851 --> 00:01:23.576
Diego Alvarez: Okay. I saw the renewal is coming up at the end of November. We'll want to get ahead of that in the next few weeks.

11
00:01:24.232 --> 00:01:32.363
Cody Barrett: Right, November 30. I'll pull in our finance folks once we get closer. Nothing dramatic on our end so far.

12
00:01:33.815 --> 00:01:37.826
Diego Alvarez: Perfect. Did you have anything you wanted to walk through today?

13
00:01:38.245 --> 00:01:45.226
Cody Barrett: Actually, there is one thing that's been bugging us for a couple weeks. It's about the tracking numbers on our site.

14
00:01:46.028 --> 00:01:47.611
Diego Alvarez: Okay, tell me more.

15
00:01:48.655 --> 00:02:01.914
Cody Barrett: So our marketing site is a single-page app, it's built in React, and we've got the Invoca tag on it. When someone lands on the homepage the dynamic number insertion swaps in the right tracking number, that part works.

16
00:02:02.362 --> 00:02:15.046
Cody Barrett: But as soon as they navigate to another page inside the app, like the pricing page or the incentives page, the number reverts back to our default number. It stops swapping.

17
00:02:16.471 --> 00:02:20.229
Diego Alvarez: So it's the route change that's tripping it up?

18
00:02:20.495 --> 00:02:33.100
Cody Barrett: That's what it looks like. The page doesn't do a full reload, so the number goes back to the main line and we lose the campaign attribution on those calls.

19
00:02:33.498 --> 00:02:35.834
Diego Alvarez: How long has this been happening?

20
00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:47.341
Cody Barrett: About two, maybe three weeks. It started around when we pushed the new site build. Our dev, Kenji, noticed it first when he was testing the Phoenix landing pages.

21
00:02:47.905 --> 00:02:51.052
Diego Alvarez: And is it every route, or just certain pages?

22
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:57.759
Cody Barrett: It seems like every internal navigation does it. The initial load is fine, then anything after that resets.

23
00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:01.080
Diego Alvarez: What's the impact you're seeing?

24
00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:10.862
Cody Barrett: Honestly it's medium for us. The landing page calls still attribute fine, but the deeper in-app navigations are getting lumped into the default source.

25
00:03:10.862 --> 00:03:13.882
Cody Barrett: Maybe 15, 20 percent of our paid calls.

26
00:03:15.119 --> 00:03:23.977
Diego Alvarez: Okay. I want to get the details right, so a few questions. Does it happen on both Google Ads and Meta traffic?

27
00:03:24.782 --> 00:03:29.905
Cody Barrett: Both. And it's across all three states, so it's not a regional thing.

28
00:03:30.136 --> 00:03:39.063
Diego Alvarez: Got it. Can you send me a couple of example calls where the number was wrong, plus the timestamp and the path the visitor took?

29
00:03:40.311 --> 00:03:44.606
Cody Barrett: Yeah, I can have Kenji pull that today. He's got the session replays.

30
00:03:45.007 --> 00:03:53.676
Diego Alvarez: That would help a lot. I'll take it to our engineering team and see what they say. I don't want to guess at what's causing it.

31
00:03:54.736 --> 00:04:00.478
Cody Barrett: Makes sense. No rush on our side, just wanted to flag it while it's fresh.

32
00:04:01.253 --> 00:04:07.901
Diego Alvarez: Appreciate it. Meanwhile, how's everything else looking? The Salesforce sync and the offline conversions to Google Ads.

33
00:04:09.013 --> 00:04:12.061
Cody Barrett: Those are fine. No complaints from the ops team.

34
00:04:12.839 --> 00:04:22.335
Diego Alvarez: Good. I'll follow up once I hear back from engineering, probably within a couple of days. And let's get a renewal planning call on the calendar for early November.

35
00:04:23.037 --> 00:04:28.527
Cody Barrett: Sounds good. Send me a couple of slots next week, mornings work best.

36
00:04:28.789 --> 00:04:31.343
Diego Alvarez: Will do. Thanks, Cody. Talk soon.

37
00:04:32.299 --> 00:04:34.946
Cody Barrett: Thanks, Diego. Have a good one.
